---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const articles = [
  {
    slug: "how-do-you-take-care-of-plants",
    title: "How Do You Take Care of Plants? Beginner Guide",
    desc: "The five basics every houseplant needs — light, water, soil, pot, and a little patience — explained without jargon.",
  },
  {
    slug: "snake-plant-for-beginners",
    title: "Snake Plant for Beginners",
    desc: "Why the snake plant is the classic first houseplant, and how to keep your first one alive.",
  },
  {
    slug: "indoor-plants-for-beginners",
    title: "Indoor Plants for Beginners: 10 Easy Picks",
    desc: "Ten plants that tolerate a beginner's learning curve, with light and watering needs for each.",
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Beginner Plant Care"
  description="Start here if you've never kept a plant alive — the basics of light, watering, and choosing your first houseplants."
  canonicalPath="/guides/beginner-plant-care/"
>
  <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <ol>
      <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
      <li><a href="/guides/">Guides</a></li>
      <li aria-current="page">Beginner Plant Care</li>
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <h1>Beginner plant care</h1>
  <p class="prose">
    If you have never kept a plant alive, start here. The guides in this
    section assume no prior knowledge: they explain what a plant needs, in
    plain language, and in the order you actually need it. The short version
    is this — give the plant enough light, water it only when the soil needs
    it, and put it in a pot that drains. Everything else is refinement.
  </p>

  <h2>Start with one forgiving plant</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    A snake plant or a pothos is a good first plant because both tolerate
    imperfect watering and a range of light. Buy one plant, learn its rhythm
    for a month, and only then add a second. Ten plants at once means ten
    different watering schedules to learn — and that is how beginners get
    overwhelmed.
  </p>

  <div class="card-grid">
    {
      articles.map((a) => (
        <article class="card">
          <h3>
            <a href={`/guides/${a.slug}/`}>{a.title}</a>
          </h3>
          <p>{a.desc}</p>
        </article>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <p>
    Also useful: <a href="/guides/plants-that-grow-in-the-dark/">Plants That Grow in the Dark (Low Light)</a> — if your home
    does not get much sun, these are the plants to start with.
  </p>
  <p>
    Not sure what is wrong with a plant you already own? Try the
    <a href="/tools/plant-problem-solver/">Plant Problem Solver</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
